          {
            "url": "https://www.forbes.com/sites/kenrapoza/2018/12/17/chinas-new-growing-pains-not-all-because-of-trade-war/",
            "title": "China's New Growing Pains Not All Due To Trump's Trade War",
            "snippets": [
              "# China's New Growing Pains Not All Due To Trump's Trade War\nEmployees at UBTech Robotics in Shenzhen, China, on November 15, 2018. China is moving higher up the... [+] value chain. Its manufacturing is becoming automated. Its corporations are moving their own manufacturing offshore, just like the Americans did nearly 40 years ago.",
              "China’s economy is going through a serious structural change. It’s changing the face of manufacturing and labor markets there at a time when President Trump is waging a trade war against China. Despite those tougher conditions for Chinese exports, many of which are made as contractors building and assembling for American brands, the China slowdown has more to do with a quickly modernizing economy than weakness in global trade",
              "In the simplest of terms, China is automating its factories. It is the largest buyer of robotic equipment. Their companies are setting up shop in Southeast Asian nations like Vietnam, building factories or contracting manufacturers abroad to do their work. It’s like old United States mill towns in the 1970s and 1980s who relocated first to cheaper producers in southern states, then later to China.",
              "ZTO Express workers prepare packages at a sorting facility in Shanghai. E-commerce is one of China’s leading industries and much of it is being automated. China’s economy is becoming less of a manufacturing one and more like the United States centered on domestic consumption.",
              "The economy is still growing, however. Third-quarter growth was—you guessed it—6.5%. That is right within Beijing’s forecast, but is down from 6.7% in the second quarter and below market consensus forecasts.\n\nIndustrial production grew 5.4% versus an estimate of 5.9% and October’s 5.9% growth rate. Fixed-asset investment in things like roads and bridges rose 5.9% versus an estimate of 5.8% and October’s 5.7%",
              "“Did anyone think it was going to be good? The market appeared surprised as evidenced by Friday’s poor equity stock performance, which one mainland broker called ‘tragic.’ December and potentially first-quarter economic data is going to get worse,” Ahern wrote in a note to clients on Friday.\n\n**Changing Manufacturing, Weaker Stimulus**",
              "In Saturday’s *New York Times* print version, reporters Keith Bradsher and Ailan Tang delved a little deeper into what’s going on with China’s manufacturing sector. The picture they offer appears to be a manufacturing recession serious enough to prompt many of China’s roughly 270 million migrant workers to head back to the hinterlands as companies reduce hours and start laying off workers",

